== Normalized concept dossiers ==

=== Formal language ===
A formal language is a mathematically specified set of finite strings over an alphabet. Grammars, regular expressions, automata, and logical syntax can define or recognize formal languages.

'''Do not conflate:''' language as a set of strings ≠ grammar that generates it; formal language ≠ natural language; formal language ≠ programming language, though programming languages have formal syntactic layers.

=== Automaton (computation) ===
A computational automaton is an abstract machine defined by states, inputs and transition rules, often used to recognize or transform formal languages.

Finite automata, pushdown automata and Turing machines occupy different computational classes.

'''Do not conflate:''' automaton ≠ finite automaton only; recognition power depends on the machine model and resources.

=== State machine ===
A state machine models behavior as a set of states plus transitions triggered by inputs/events/conditions.

State machines can be finite or infinite and can model software, hardware, protocols, workflows, control systems and formal computation.

'''Do not conflate:''' state machine ≠ finite-state machine by definition; a state is an abstract configuration, not necessarily a physical location.

=== Turing machine ===
A Turing machine is an abstract model with finite control, a read/write head, tape and transition rule. It is foundational for computability theory.

The Church–Turing thesis identifies effectively calculable functions with Turing-computable functions (or equivalent formalisms); this is a thesis about effective computation, not a theorem proved from a prior formal definition.

=== Computability ===
Computability studies which functions/problems can be solved by effective procedures under formal models of computation.

Some problems are undecidable even with unlimited time under the model.

'''Do not conflate:''' computability ≠ efficiency; decidable ≠ tractable.

=== Turing completeness ===
A computational system is commonly called Turing complete when it can simulate a universal Turing machine, subject to idealized unbounded resources.

Turing completeness says little about efficiency, ergonomics, safety, or practical usefulness.

It also does not imply that every program behavior is decidable.

=== Computational complexity ===
Computational complexity studies resource requirements of computational problems, especially time and space as functions of input size.

Complexity classes group problems by resource bounds and computational models.

'''Do not conflate:''' asymptotic complexity ≠ benchmark runtime; worst-case ≠ average-case; problem complexity ≠ one implementation's performance.

=== Computer architecture ===
Computer architecture concerns the organization and programmer-visible structure of computer systems, including instruction sets, memory hierarchy, processors, I/O and system organization.

'''Do not conflate:''' architecture ≠ microarchitecture; ISA semantics can be implemented by different microarchitectures.

=== Operating system ===
An operating system manages hardware resources and provides abstractions/services for programs and users, including processes, memory, storage, files, devices, protection and scheduling.

'''Do not conflate:''' operating system ≠ kernel only; program ≠ process.

=== Computer network ===
A computer network is a collection of interconnected computing devices that exchange data using communication links and protocols.

The Internet is a network of networks, not the definition of computer networking as a whole.

=== Database ===
A database is an organized collection of data designed for persistent storage, retrieval and management.

A DBMS is software that defines, queries, updates, protects and manages databases.

'''Do not conflate:''' database ≠ DBMS; relational database ≠ all databases.

=== Compiler ===
A compiler transforms a source representation, typically a program in one language, into an equivalent or sufficiently semantics-preserving target representation.

Compilation can include parsing, semantic analysis, optimization and code generation, but optimization is not required for something to be a compiler.

'''Do not conflate:''' compiler ≠ interpreter; compilation and interpretation can coexist in one implementation.

=== Distributed system ===
A distributed system consists of multiple autonomous computational components that communicate and coordinate to provide a service or computation.

Failures, latency, concurrency, partial information and lack of a single global clock create characteristic design problems.

'''Do not conflate:''' distributed system ≠ computer network; the network is substrate, while the distributed system includes coordinated computation/protocol/state.

=== Computer security ===
Computer security protects computational systems and information against unauthorized access, modification, disruption, disclosure and related failures.

Confidentiality, integrity and availability are central but not exhaustive security objectives; authentication, authorization, accountability and resilience are also important.

'''Do not conflate:''' security ≠ cryptography; security is a system property shaped by software, hardware, users, operations and threat models.

=== Cryptography ===
Cryptography designs mathematical techniques and protocols for security properties such as confidentiality, integrity, authentication and verifiability.

Modern cryptography includes encryption, digital signatures, message authentication, key exchange, commitments, zero-knowledge proofs and more.

'''Do not conflate:''' cryptography ≠ encryption; hashing ≠ encryption.

=== Human-computer interaction ===
Human-computer interaction (HCI) studies the design, use and evaluation of interactive computational systems for human use.

It combines computer science with psychology, design, ergonomics and social/contextual analysis.

'''Do not conflate:''' HCI ≠ graphical interface design only; usability is one dimension within a broader interaction field.

=== Esoteric programming language ===
An esoteric programming language (esolang) is designed primarily for experimentation, conceptual exploration, artistic/playful expression, unusual constraints, or challenging conventional programming-language assumptions rather than ordinary production utility.

Esolangs can illuminate language semantics, minimal computation, notation and programming culture.

'''Do not conflate:''' esolang ≠ useless by definition; esolang ≠ necessarily Turing complete.

